WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.534
-Our first guest tonight
is a talented actor

00:00:02.534 --> 00:00:04.300
and host you know from his work
on shows such as

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:06.901
"Brooklyn Nine-Nine"
and "America's Got Talent."

00:00:06.901 --> 00:00:09.267
He hosts "America's Got Talent:
All-Stars,"

00:00:09.267 --> 00:00:11.701
which airs Monday night
at 8:00 here on NBC.

00:00:11.701 --> 00:00:13.067
Let's take a look.

00:00:13.067 --> 00:00:16.501
♪ People talking without
speaking ♪

00:00:16.501 --> 00:00:18.767
-Oh, yeah!

00:00:18.767 --> 00:00:24.968
♪ People hearing
without listening ♪

00:00:24.968 --> 00:00:30.000
♪ People writing songs ♪

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.534
♪ That voices never share ♪

00:00:34.534 --> 00:00:35.534
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:35.534 --> 00:00:36.834
-Please welcome back to the show

00:00:36.834 --> 00:00:39.067
our friend Terry Crews,
everybody!

00:00:39.067 --> 00:00:40.901
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:40.901 --> 00:00:50.901
♪♪

00:00:50.901 --> 00:00:58.067
♪♪

00:00:58.067 --> 00:00:59.334
Oh, my gosh.
-Oh!

00:00:59.334 --> 00:01:00.934
-Now, I actually have to say,

00:01:00.934 --> 00:01:04.167
Terry, I think you owed us
that entrance.

00:01:04.167 --> 00:01:06.234
-I did, I did.
-Because it was --

00:01:06.234 --> 00:01:08.133
it seemed very unfair
that the clip we used

00:01:08.133 --> 00:01:10.868
to introduce you was
someone else being incredible,

00:01:10.868 --> 00:01:12.200
and all we watched you do
is go...

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:14.767
-Is go, "Yeah!"
[ Laughter ]

00:01:14.767 --> 00:01:17.534
No, that was -- this is --
I love that show.

00:01:17.534 --> 00:01:19.767
I love "AGT," man.
-Yeah, man.

00:01:19.767 --> 00:01:22.200
-I love being the host.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.400
First of all, I study success.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.567
I study what it's about because,
you know, I failed a lot.

00:01:27.567 --> 00:01:30.467
-Mm-hmm.
-I study, like, what works.

00:01:30.467 --> 00:01:32.934
And when I watch these guys
just come in here

00:01:32.934 --> 00:01:35.067
and do these miracles every day.
And I realize --

00:01:35.067 --> 00:01:36.734
Because people think
it's about competition.

00:01:36.734 --> 00:01:39.968
But these guys
never look at the competition.

00:01:39.968 --> 00:01:42.501
All they do
is worry about themselves.

00:01:42.501 --> 00:01:45.000
When you're seeing somebody
like Tom Ball,

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.501
we gave him, like, this giant --

00:01:46.501 --> 00:01:48.467
like, all of us gave him
a Golden Buzzer.

00:01:48.467 --> 00:01:51.968
He wasn't worried about anything
else but what he was doing.

00:01:51.968 --> 00:01:53.734
And I realized
that's what I need to do.

00:01:53.734 --> 00:01:54.934
-Yeah.
-Because every time

00:01:54.934 --> 00:01:58.934
I've ever failed,
you are looking at the side,

00:01:58.934 --> 00:02:00.267
and you're looking
at other people,

00:02:00.267 --> 00:02:01.601
and you're seeing what
everybody else is doing.

00:02:01.601 --> 00:02:04.367
But when it works,
you just work on yourself.

00:02:04.367 --> 00:02:06.000
And that's what I love
about this whole thing.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.133
-And now, explain real quick
the all-stars spinoff

00:02:09.133 --> 00:02:10.200
of the show here.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:12.033
These are people
from previous seasons?

00:02:12.033 --> 00:02:14.300
These are across the world?
-Exactly.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:17.667
The past winners
and the most memorable acts.

00:02:17.667 --> 00:02:21.100
And this is what's so wonderful.
It's voted on by the superfans.

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:24.300
And the superfans are like,
you know, people from

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:26.467
every different state,
in all 50 states,

00:02:26.467 --> 00:02:29.567
who vote on "AGT."
So this is a good --

00:02:29.567 --> 00:02:32.267
The great thing is that
the judges don't matter.

00:02:32.267 --> 00:02:34.901
Whatever Simon says,
whatever Howie says,

00:02:34.901 --> 00:02:36.968
whatever Heidi says,
it's just their opinion,

00:02:36.968 --> 00:02:40.067
but it does not go into --
-I mean, I want to live

00:02:40.067 --> 00:02:41.868
in a world where nothing
Simon says matters.

00:02:41.868 --> 00:02:44.033
[ Laughter ]
-I know!

00:02:44.033 --> 00:02:45.934
Believe you me,
that's why I'm glad I host.

00:02:45.934 --> 00:02:47.234
I don't ever judge.

00:02:47.234 --> 00:02:48.868
I'm just like --
I'm always there.

00:02:48.868 --> 00:02:51.033
I'm there for the acts.
-But you do get a Golden Buzzer.

00:02:51.033 --> 00:02:52.434
-I do.
-And this is your chance

00:02:52.434 --> 00:02:54.133
to put someone through.

00:02:54.133 --> 00:02:55.734
And you used it recently.

00:02:55.734 --> 00:02:58.434
Explain who you used it for
and that moment

00:02:58.434 --> 00:02:59.968
where you decided,
"I'm gonna hit this thing."

00:02:59.968 --> 00:03:06.033
-I gave the Detroit Youth Choir
their second Golden Buzzer

00:03:06.033 --> 00:03:11.167
from me because these kids, we
come from similar circumstances.

00:03:11.167 --> 00:03:13.033
I mean, you know,
I'm from Flint, Michigan.

00:03:13.033 --> 00:03:15.000
These guys are from Detroit.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:18.400
They've seen so many obstacles
in their lives.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:21.667
And the thing is --
it's just like I said before.

00:03:21.667 --> 00:03:24.033
They never worried about
their circumstances.

00:03:24.033 --> 00:03:25.934
They just worked on themselves.

00:03:25.934 --> 00:03:28.501
And these kids
have made a miracle.

00:03:28.501 --> 00:03:29.901
You know, what's so wonderful,

00:03:29.901 --> 00:03:31.467
the first time
they got the Golden Buzzer,

00:03:31.467 --> 00:03:34.767
they did not win the show,
but they got $1 million

00:03:34.767 --> 00:03:36.701
from the city of Detroit.
-Wow, there you go.

00:03:36.701 --> 00:03:38.968
-And I mean, it was fantastic.

00:03:38.968 --> 00:03:41.167
So, you know,
this Golden Buzzer thing

00:03:41.167 --> 00:03:44.234
has real power
to change people's lives.

00:03:44.234 --> 00:03:47.901
And so on "All-Stars," I gave
them another Golden Buzzer.

00:03:47.901 --> 00:03:49.067
-[ Laughs ]
-You know what I mean?

00:03:49.067 --> 00:03:50.901
I was like, "I'm gonna
give y'all another one!"

00:03:50.901 --> 00:03:52.801
[ Cheers and applause ]
You know what I mean?

00:03:52.801 --> 00:03:55.334
-You talked -- I want to
talk about your upbringing

00:03:55.334 --> 00:03:57.300
real quick in your new book.
But you also --

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:58.934
I know you grew up
in Flint, Michigan.

00:03:58.934 --> 00:04:00.901
And you took your first sort of

00:04:00.901 --> 00:04:02.434
winter vacation
with your family.

00:04:02.434 --> 00:04:03.567
-Yeah.
-You had never really done

00:04:03.567 --> 00:04:05.501
the winter vacation.
-I've never done it.

00:04:05.501 --> 00:04:07.567
You know, 'cause first of all,
growing up broke,

00:04:07.567 --> 00:04:09.968
you know, you're like, "Cold?
Why do y'all go to the cold?

00:04:09.968 --> 00:04:11.267
What is that?"
-Yeah.

00:04:11.267 --> 00:04:13.200
-You know what I mean?
I used to sit in my basement

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.000
and wait for summer
like for three months.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:16.300
[ Laughter ]

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:17.767
I'd watch all
the "Gilligan's Island,"

00:04:17.767 --> 00:04:18.901
then I'd get out,
and I was like,

00:04:18.901 --> 00:04:20.667
"Oh, man, it's summer,"
you know?

00:04:20.667 --> 00:04:23.767
But one thing I realized --
I was like, "Oh, man.

00:04:23.767 --> 00:04:25.601
This is what
they're talking about.

00:04:25.601 --> 00:04:28.934
Like, s'mores and tubing
and, like --"

00:04:28.934 --> 00:04:30.000
You know what I mean?

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.300
White-people money!
-Yeah.

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:32.734
[ Laughter ]
-You know what I'm saying?

00:04:32.734 --> 00:04:34.868
I was like,
"That's what they're doing!

00:04:34.868 --> 00:04:37.400
That's what they mean."
-Was it so exciting to know

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:38.968
you were making s'mores money?

00:04:38.968 --> 00:04:41.367
[ Laughter ]
-Yes, it was.

00:04:41.367 --> 00:04:43.267
-I know you've excelled at
a lot of sports in your life,

00:04:43.267 --> 00:04:45.734
but safe to say it's a little
too late to take up skiing?

00:04:45.734 --> 00:04:47.601
-It's very too late, yes.
-Okay. Gotcha.

00:04:47.601 --> 00:04:49.300
-No, I'm not about
to break anything.

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:51.334
-Yeah, that's a very --
I don't blame you for that.

00:04:51.334 --> 00:04:53.868
Now, I've always been impressed
with the fact that you are also

00:04:53.868 --> 00:04:56.434
an accomplished author, and this
is a book for adolescents.

00:04:56.434 --> 00:04:58.467
And the name, I feel like
it's crazy to me

00:04:58.467 --> 00:05:02.133
you hadn't already used the name
about this group of kids.

00:05:02.133 --> 00:05:04.434
It's "Terry's Crew."
-Yeah.

00:05:04.434 --> 00:05:05.767
[ Laughter ]

00:05:05.767 --> 00:05:07.634
-I mean that one -- did you --
have you had that one

00:05:07.634 --> 00:05:09.801
in your back pocket for a while?
-A long time.

00:05:09.801 --> 00:05:11.567
-Yeah, there you go.
-A long time.

00:05:11.567 --> 00:05:13.901
You know, you try to work out
all these things that

00:05:13.901 --> 00:05:16.334
have meaning in your life,
but this is the thing for me.

00:05:16.334 --> 00:05:20.100
The reason one thing that
I survived and made it through

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:22.801
is because of the people
who were in my life.

00:05:22.801 --> 00:05:26.334
This book is based on
real people who blessed me.

00:05:26.334 --> 00:05:29.601
I actually dedicated this
to my art teacher

00:05:29.601 --> 00:05:32.367
who actually filled out
all these applications for me

00:05:32.367 --> 00:05:34.400
when I didn't even
believe in myself.

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:36.801
And he got me
two different scholarships.

00:05:36.801 --> 00:05:38.100
And it's one of those things
where --

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:39.934
[ Cheers and applause ]
Yes.

00:05:39.934 --> 00:05:44.801
You realize that --
No man is an island.

00:05:44.801 --> 00:05:47.801
Like, the whole self-made man
is a myth.

00:05:47.801 --> 00:05:51.000
It's really about who believes
in you, who's with you,

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:53.834
and who -- and the right circle
that you're in

00:05:53.834 --> 00:05:56.033
that'll allow you to do
anything you want to do.

00:05:56.033 --> 00:05:57.501
-And you came --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:57.501 --> 00:05:59.100
-You came
from a tough neighborhood.

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:02.834
And it wasn't just teachers.
It was sometimes maybe people

00:06:02.834 --> 00:06:04.467
who, from the outside,
can be seen as

00:06:04.467 --> 00:06:05.934
the rougher element
of the neighborhood.

00:06:05.934 --> 00:06:09.901
-Listen, I had to avoid,
you know, crack dealers,

00:06:09.901 --> 00:06:11.834
gang members, the whole thing.

00:06:11.834 --> 00:06:14.334
You know, I had to dumb myself
down just to get into school.

00:06:14.334 --> 00:06:16.834
I was like, "Yeah, man, I don't
know nothing about that stuff."

00:06:16.834 --> 00:06:18.133
[ Laughter ]
And they're like, "Yeah, okay,

00:06:18.133 --> 00:06:19.801
you cool."
But then I'd go back in.

00:06:19.801 --> 00:06:22.334
"Hey, did you see 'Star Wars'?
It's so amazing!"

00:06:22.334 --> 00:06:24.434
I'm like this big nerd,
but I had to hide it

00:06:24.434 --> 00:06:26.701
and the whole thing.
And it's wild because

00:06:26.701 --> 00:06:28.067
when you find people

00:06:28.067 --> 00:06:30.467
you can be yourself around,
no matter what --

00:06:30.467 --> 00:06:32.234
You know, we were a group
of Black nerds,

00:06:32.234 --> 00:06:33.801
and we would sit around
and just talk about

00:06:33.801 --> 00:06:35.367
all these wonderful things.

00:06:35.367 --> 00:06:38.501
And finally, you realize, man,
this is what it's about.

00:06:38.501 --> 00:06:41.400
It's really about just -- it's
not about impressing everybody

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:44.133
but finding that crew
that you can --

00:06:44.133 --> 00:06:46.667
that really believe in you and
will take you to the next level.

00:06:46.667 --> 00:06:48.267
-It does seem like --
I mean, you're talking about

00:06:48.267 --> 00:06:50.467
yourself as a nerd.
And, again, I will just say

00:06:50.467 --> 00:06:52.200
this is not -- in my
high school, this is not --

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:55.167
you would not have been a nerd.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:55.167 --> 00:06:57.534
So, wait --
you were a man of many, uh,

00:06:57.534 --> 00:07:00.734
sort of many different skills.
-What you gotta notice is that

00:07:00.734 --> 00:07:03.634
I hadn't really grown into
my hands and ears

00:07:03.634 --> 00:07:05.400
and feet at that time.
-Okay. Gotcha.

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:07.567
-You know, I finally grew
into all this stuff.

00:07:07.567 --> 00:07:09.000
You know what I mean?
But I was trying to look cool.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:10.267
You know what I mean?
I was like,

00:07:10.267 --> 00:07:13.267
"I can palm the basketball."
I wasn't very good.

00:07:13.267 --> 00:07:14.868
-You had a --
you recently were out.

00:07:14.868 --> 00:07:17.100
And you were recognized
by a young fan...

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:19.667
-Yes.
-...who took time to do

00:07:19.667 --> 00:07:21.100
a drawing of you while you were
at the restaurant, right?

00:07:21.100 --> 00:07:22.767
-Yes.
-And then this was

00:07:22.767 --> 00:07:24.734
brought over for you.
-It was amazing.

00:07:24.734 --> 00:07:27.634
This 9-year-old girl --
I walked in the restaurant.

00:07:27.634 --> 00:07:29.167
This is when we were in Utah.
It was crazy.

00:07:29.167 --> 00:07:30.467
I walked in the restaurant.

00:07:30.467 --> 00:07:34.267
This girl grabbed her crayons
and started immediately.

00:07:34.267 --> 00:07:36.734
She came over nervously
with my family,

00:07:36.734 --> 00:07:38.167
and we're eating
and the whole thing.

00:07:38.167 --> 00:07:41.868
And she showed me this picture,
and it blessed me so much.

00:07:41.868 --> 00:07:43.033
She was like, "I'm so sorry.

00:07:43.033 --> 00:07:45.033
The shoes
were supposed to be shinier."

00:07:45.033 --> 00:07:46.167
-Well, I think
she did a great job.

00:07:46.167 --> 00:07:47.968
-Look at this!
It's so cute!

00:07:47.968 --> 00:07:49.133
-And I mean --
[ Applause ]

00:07:49.133 --> 00:07:52.467
-I'm framing this.
It means so much to me.

00:07:52.467 --> 00:07:54.667
-And let me just say --
-It looks just like me!

00:07:54.667 --> 00:07:56.567
-Looks just like --
And she got the hands right.

00:07:56.567 --> 00:07:57.734
[ Laughter ]

00:07:57.734 --> 00:07:59.200
Those are those big hands
that were grabbing

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:00.934
the basketballs earlier.
So look at that.

00:08:00.934 --> 00:08:03.100
-Her name is Belle.
Hey, Belle, thank you...

00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:04.267
-Well done.
-...for this beautiful picture.

00:08:04.267 --> 00:08:06.734
-Well done, Belle.
And, Terry, it's always

00:08:06.734 --> 00:08:09.133
such a pleasure to see you, man.
-Always. Always.

00:08:09.133 --> 00:08:10.868
-"America's Got Talent:
All-Stars,"

00:08:10.868 --> 00:08:12.834
Monday nights at 8:00
here on NBC.

00:08:12.834 --> 00:08:14.968
We'll be right back
with Andrew Dismukes.

